{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Speak to the children of Israel and say to them.</b>" "<b>Adam.</b> To include converts, for they accept the covenant like Israel. And to include even a גר תושב (resident alien), even though 'adam' here implies Israel. Nevertheless, since after it is written 'מכם,' which implies 'from you' and not from other nations — this constitutes a מיעוט אחר מיעוט (a restriction after a restriction), and a מיעוט אחר מיעוט serves only to include. Thus it includes גר תושב. And so we say in general: 'איש איש' — to include גר תושב, for they make vows and freewill offerings like Israel." "<b>When he offers.</b> After the Tabernacle was erected, it explains to you how His servants are to serve Him in His house." "<b>From you.</b> And not all of you — to exclude the heretics, who do not accept the covenant like Israel." "<b>From you.</b> In you I have apportioned it, and not among the nations. For even a wicked גר תושב — offerings are accepted from him, in order to draw him near under the wings of the Divine Presence. 'אדם כי יקריב' — restrict it: 'אדם מכם כי יקריב.'" "<b>When he offers.</b> When he consecrates." "<b>An offering to YHWH</b> — a general statement; <b>from the animal, from the cattle and from the flock</b> — a specification; <b>you shall offer your offering</b> — it returned and made a general statement. There is in the general statement only what is in the specification, to exclude the wild animal, which is not offered, because it was not included in the blessing in the act of Creation, on account of the serpent that was worthy of a curse, and one who brings it transgresses. Another interpretation: we learn from here the humility of the Holy One, blessed be He, that He did not burden His children except with something available in their hands, and as it is written, ״O my people, what have I done to you, and wherein have I wearied you?״ etc. Another interpretation: <b>from the animal, from the cattle and from the flock</b> — and not from man; and because the Canaanites burn their sons and daughters to the Most High, this verse was stated."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {1 "Resolved א״י as גר תושב based on context: the argument turns on whether non-Israelites may bring offerings, and the citation 'איש איש' with the phrase 'נודרים נדרים ונדבות כישראל' confirms this reading. The abbreviation א״י could also point to ארץ ישראל but that does not fit the argument."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:09:43.738668741Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}